<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

  <!-- Compact styles for data-entry windows: mass edit, track details, the rip and vinyl track
       lists, and the release lookup. A 32 px input suits a settings page with a few controls. In a
       tag editor the user needs to see many fields at once and spot an empty or wrong one without
       scrolling. These styles make the inputs 24 px tall and the text 12 pt, so more of a record
       fits on screen.

       Merge this into a dialog's own Resources, not App.xaml. Uno-Skia resolves a template's
       {ThemeResource} against the control's ancestor Resources. A dialog scope is what makes the
       styles take effect, and it limits the change to data-entry windows. The rest of the app keeps
       its normal size. (RpmAccentControls.xaml is merged per screen for the same reason.)

       Inline controls shrink with the fields (DenseButtonStyle in Generic.xaml). The footer buttons
       do not: Cancel and Apply are the window's own actions and keep the normal 32 px. -->

  <!-- TextBox and PasswordBox. The 24 px height is applied to the template's BorderElement, which
       reads TextControlThemeMinHeight directly. Setting MinHeight on the control alone has no effect.
       The padding is the stock 10,5,6,6, adjusted to centre a 12 pt line in a 24 px box. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="TextControlThemeMinHeight">24</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="TextControlThemeMinWidth">0</x:Double>
  <Thickness x:Key="TextControlThemePadding">8,2,6,3</Thickness>

  <!-- ComboBox, closed and editable. The editable variant hosts a TextBox, so it also uses the
       resources above. Its own padding leaves room on the right for the drop-down chevron. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="ComboBoxMinHeight">24</x:Double>
  <x:Double x:Key="ComboBoxMinWidth">0</x:Double>
  <Thickness x:Key="ComboBoxPadding">9,2,0,3</Thickness>
  <Thickness x:Key="ComboBoxEditableTextPadding">8,2,30,3</Thickness>

  <!-- Text size for every control in the scope. The drop-down list keeps the framework's row height,
       because an open list is a click target and not a form field. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="ControlContentThemeFontSize">12</x:Double>

  <!-- A tick beside a field is a marker, not a labelled control, so it gets no 120 px reserve. -->
  <x:Double x:Key="CheckBoxMinWidth">0</x:Double>

</ResourceDictionary>
